  "rationale": "Spec: throughput >= 1 MSPS, max_abs_err <= 2^-10, minimise luts_plus_ffs and maximise accuracy_bits. Only iterative and unrolled_k are available. unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations per cycle, multiplying datapath area and reducing Fmax; its only benefit is fewer cycles, but the throughput constraint is already easily met by iterative at the required N values. Therefore the feasible Pareto front must come from iterative. The first exploration should map that front broadly: data_width 11..28 covers the minimum feasible output width up to the registry maximum; n_iter 11..30 covers the minimum CORDIC accuracy up to the registry maximum; angle_guard -2..4 and frac_guard 0..4 cover the guard-bit trade-offs; both roundings are included because rounding can save area at the error threshold. This focuses the full 100-eval budget on the only family that can produce non-dominated points for this spec."
}

{
  "rationale": "Final round: only `stop` or `infeasible` are admissible, and the evidence rules out infeasible. The spec's constraints are both met with margin by many designs: 0% of evaluations violate throughput_msps >= 1 (best seen 13.5 MSPS) and only 13% violate max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (best seen 2^-24.17). The feasible Pareto front already spans the area objective from luts_plus_ffs=322 up to 675 (HV ref 1500) and accuracy from 12.8 to 24.2 bits (HV ref 10), i.e. it covers both objective ranges well past their reference points, giving hypervolume 1.461e+04. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is satisfied by the front's cheapest point, iterative [data_width=18 n_iter=15 angle_guard=0 frac_guard=2 rounding=trunc] at luts_plus_ffs=322, accuracy_bits=12.8 (max_abs_err 2^-12.75, comfortably under the 2^-10 limit) and throughput 10.8 MSPS (well above 1 MSPS). No constraint is unreachable, so `infeasible` is not justified, and with the round cap reached there is no scope to improve the front further.",
